{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/457615-updating-hollywood-syndrome-to-2e","id":457615,"title":"Updating hollywood syndrome to 2e","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-03-25T19:35:18-07:00","id":457615,"title":"Updating hollywood syndrome to 2e","content":"So in my research on werewolf folklore, I've discovered that some cultures belived that werewolves in urhan form could be identified by the fact that they had natural bobtails and I've read a cryptozoology book in which the wolfmen sighted by a couple in Maine had bobtails. In 1e I imagine that a werewolf that had a bobtail, and thus could be distingished from a normal wolf by the knowing would be a varient of the Hollywood syndrome flaw. But how would this be represented in 2e?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Despite the user name I am actually bigender.<br /> <br /> My Savannah Setting for CofD can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/959455-the-young-man-in-the-cafe-s-savannah-georgia-setting-writeup\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> <br /> My heroes as monster tamers rules for BtP can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/975230-heroes-as-monster-tamers-rules-ideas\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3932-the-young-man-in-the-cafe","name":"The young man in the cafe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3932_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-25T20:15:30-07:00","id":457676,"title":"","content":"Probably some sort of persistent condition, I assume. Gain a Beat when your bobtail gets you in trouble, or something like that?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-26T13:33:02-07:00","id":458318,"title":"","content":"That's what I thought, I just wanted to be sure. Apparently, natural bob tails have become a bit of a controversy in the dog fancier world. It seems that some breeders have been introducing the mutated bobtail gene into breeds that normally have their tails docked, in order to get around laws in some countries that ban the procedure. The problem is that the mutation (which is semi dominant) is lethal when homozygous, meaning that breeding two dogs with the mutation together will result (with a litter of four, as an example) in one dog that dies, with one of the remaining dogs being born with a normal tail and won't be desirable to people who want that breed, so only two dogs in the litter will have the natural bobtails. As a result, some dog fanciers have been arguing that the natural bob tail mutation isn't an ethical replacement for tail docking.<br /> <br /> I imagine this whole controversy could be given a twisted mirror in the breeding programs of the Ivory Claws (Assuming that the werewolf gene for bobtails works the same way and that the local Ivory Claws were selecting for that trait for whatever reason.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Despite the user name I am actually bigender.<br /> <br /> My Savannah Setting for CofD can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world